From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:55:05 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox: run the Claude agent adapters inside ai-jail,
 set from Settings

Running the Claude agent jailed meant hand-writing a second agent_servers
entry whose command was `ai-jail ... claude-agent-acp-plus`, keeping its
maps and hosts in sync by hand, and nothing in Zeo said whether a thread
was jailed at all. A forgotten flag (no --map .git) left .git/hooks
writable, which runs code on the host at the next commit. This patch makes
the jail a setting: Settings > AI > Agent Sandbox (ai-jail), per project,
and Zeo builds the ai-jail command itself. Story 043.

What it adds:

  * crate agent_sandbox: settings `agent_sandbox` {enabled (default false),
    ai_jail_path, claude_config_dir (~/.claude-jailed), rw_maps,
    allow_hosts, projects{<root>: enabled/rw_maps/allow_hosts}} and
    `agent_memory` {enabled, endpoint}; validation; the ai-jail version
    probe (>= 2.4.0, else a GHSA warning; --forward-port detected from
    --help); the command generator; the launch decision; the confirmation
    prompts; an ai-memory MCP client (status, recent pages, forget).
  * agent_servers: a spawn hook. For a local project with the sandbox on,
    a Claude adapter (claude-agent-acp-plus or claude-agent-tui, matched by
    id, file name, resolved path, shell words or the @lucascouts package)
    starts as `ai-jail --claude-dir <dir> --map <root>/.git --map
    <root>/.zed --rw-map ... --allow-host ... --env NAME ... -- <agent>`,
    with the canonical project root as its cwd. The `--` keeps an agent
    argument that is also a jail flag from being read by ai-jail.
  * When ai-memory's port is forwarded, the agent runs behind a no_proxy
    wrapper: `-- /bin/sh -c '<export no_proxy/NO_PROXY; exec "$@">' sh
    <agent>`. ai-jail's filtered egress exports its own proxy and empties
    no_proxy after every --env, so loopback would go to the proxy; the
    exclusion (loopback plus any configured no_proxy) travels in
    ZEO_NO_PROXY, forwarded by name and unset before exec. Inside the
    jail loopback reaches only ai-jail's own bridges, so this opens no
    route the proxy guards. The jail therefore needs /bin/sh.
  * agent_ui: a lock icon on the thread toolbar (jailed, off, own command,
    outdated, settings changed since start); palette commands `agent
    sandbox: toggle`, `agent memory: status`, `agent memory: open project
    memory`. settings_ui: the Agent Sandbox and Agent Memory pages, with
    the generated command as a preview. Every control is reachable with
    Tab, and the page scrolls the focused control into view. The toolbar
    lock is not a Tab stop, like upstream's own sandbox icon.

Security boundary -- the off switch stays out of the agent's reach:

  * Both settings exist only in the user file; the same key in a
    project's .zed/settings.json reaches no value (Zeo logs a warning).
  * ai-jail's private home and /run tmpfs hide ~/.config/zeo and
    $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (settings.json, the devtools bridge socket, D-Bus).
  * <root>/.git and <root>/.zed are mapped read-only by absolute path, so
    the agent can neither plant a hook nor project settings unjailed Zeo
    would act on. ~/.claude stays unmapped (claude_config_dir instead).
  * Writable folders are refused when they re-expose a protected path:
    the config dir, ~/.claude, ~/.claude.json, ~/.ssh, ~/.gnupg, /, home,
    $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, and every target a symlink inside them points to
    (the walk fails closed past its limits); also refused: nested maps,
    a ':' or non-UTF-8 path (ai-jail reads --map as SOURCE:DEST), and a
    project root with either (cause=root_unusable).
  * Turning the jail off, from the page, the list or the palette, always
    asks first; Cancel is the first, preselected button, so a stray Enter
    keeps it on. Turning it on does not ask.
  * Only env names configured for the agent (its settings env, the
    registry/npx distribution env) are forwarded, by name and never as
    NAME=VALUE on argv; ai-jail's own environment is cleared to Zeo's
    PATH/LANG/XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/HOME (plus ZEO_NO_PROXY when ai-memory is
    forwarded), so shell proxies, tokens and AI_JAIL_*/BWRAP_BIN never
    reach or steer it. A shell-only
    ANTHROPIC_API_KEY must move into the agent's settings env.
  * The ai-memory token goes only into an HTTP header, never on argv or in
    a log; only a loopback endpoint's port is forwarded into the jail.

Fail closed: with the sandbox on and no usable jail (ai-jail missing or
unreadable, an invalid setting, a symlinked .git/.zed, a missing root),
the agent does not start. The thread shows the reason and "The agent was
not started without the sandbox."; there is no unjailed fallback.

Scope: the Claude adapters only. Zed's native agent, other ACP agents
and MCP servers Zeo starts are not wrapped. Remote projects are
unchanged. An entry whose command is already ai-jail is left alone and
shown as "jailed by its own command".

Known gaps:

  * terminal_auth_task (the agent's login command) runs unjailed.
  * The agent can create sub/.git, or .git when the project has none,
    with hooks inside the writable project; they run when the user
    commits there.
  * Any binary named ai-jail counts as the agent's own sandbox.
  * An ai-jail upgrade or PATH change after start (binary drift) is not
    shown on the icon; settings and memory drift are.

Packaging: USE ai-jail (default off), its own series group after
devtools-bridge and before test, marked `# @default: off` so the portable
builds (zeo-bin, .deb/.rpm, AppImage, Flatpak) leave it out. New crate
agent_sandbox; no new third-party dependency.

Tests: agent_sandbox unit and gpui tests (validation hostile and benign,
argv shape, version probe, launch decision, prompts, scope resolution,
ai-memory fixtures) plus tests/real_ai_jail.rs against a real ai-jail;
agent_servers spawn-hook and lifecycle tests; agent_ui icon tests;
settings_ui page tests.

The lifecycle tests also hold 0061's: the jail test requires ZEO_THREAD both
in ai-jail's own environment and as `--env ZEO_THREAD`, since ai-jail passes
on only the names given that way, and unjailed_agent_carries_the_zeo_thread_marker
checks an unjailed start. They live here because this fixture is the one that
spawns a real agent script and reads back its environment (story 050).

ACP v2 (0.6.1_p20261021). Upstream fae8a35482 moved `connect` into acp.rs;
with acp-beta on -- 0001 forces it in every Zeo build -- it tries ACP v2
first and falls back to v1 when the agent refuses the version. Its v2 attempt
spawned through the login shell, in the project's first folder, with Zeo's
whole environment, and dropped the launch's io_ended signal: a jailed start
leaked SHELL and every other variable into ai-jail, and the launch registry
lost its record while the agent still ran. AcpV2Connection::stdio_with now
takes the same StdioLaunch as v1 -- the override cwd, a cleared env, no login
shell. The v2 attempt holds an end signal of its own, forwarded to the
launch's only when the v2 connection is kept; on a v1 fallback the refused
process is dropped and the record follows the v1 process. Tests:
sandbox_hook_v2_tests -- a v2 start spawns once, in the root, with the
cleared env, and its record ends with the connection; a v1 fallback spawns
twice, both cleared, the refused process ends and the record follows the
live one.

a5c4ca8b review (2026-10-25, story 061): upstream's only Linux sandbox change
is in AcpThread::create_terminal, which builds the shell non_interactive() when a
sandbox_wrap is present. That wrap is Zed's own sandbox for the native agent's
terminal tool (passed from agent.rs, applied in acp_thread's terminal.rs); the
terminal and terminal_view diffs only move to Modifiers::secondary(cx). This
patch jails the adapter process at spawn and leaves the native agent out of
scope, so the two never meet: argv, cleared env, v2 spawn and fail-closed are
unchanged. Terminals an ACP agent asks Zeo to create still run outside the jail,
as before. No code change needed.
---

